  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force tries every starting position, counting zeros in an inner loop and breaking when they exceed k. The optimal uses a grow-shrink sliding window: count zeros in the current window and advance the left edge when the count exceeds k.",
    "why_optimal": "Brute force is O(n^2) because each new start re-scans from scratch. The sliding window is O(n) since each index enters and leaves once. On 22 cases the optimal ran in 0.11 ms versus 0.42 ms (~3.7x faster).",
    "pitfalls": "Don't reset left to right when shrinking\u2014advance it incrementally. The window length grows monotonically even as left slides; record best at every right step to catch the true maximum."
  },
